1. What are the commonly used uninformed search algorithms?
•	The commonly used uninformed search algorithms are:
o	Depth-First Search (DFS)
o	Breadth-First Search (BFS)
o	Uniform Cost Search (UCS)
Uninformed search algorithms don't have any additional information about the goal (such as heuristics) and explore the search space based solely on the structure of the graph or tree.
2. Which data structure is used in BFS?
•	Queue is the data structure used in Breadth-First Search (BFS).
•	BFS follows a First-In-First-Out (FIFO) strategy, where nodes are processed in the order they are discovered. The queue holds the nodes to be explored next and ensures that nodes at the current depth level are explored before moving on to the next level.
3. Which data structure is used in DFS?
•	Stack (or recursion in its recursive form) is used in Depth-First Search (DFS).
•	DFS follows a Last-In-First-Out (LIFO) strategy, where it explores as far down a branch as possible before backtracking. The stack keeps track of the nodes to be explored, ensuring that the most recently discovered node is processed next.
4. What is the performance measure for DFS and BFS?
•	The performance measures for DFS and BFS are:
o	Time Complexity:
	For both DFS and BFS, the time complexity is O(V + E), where V is the number of vertices (nodes) and E is the number of edges in the graph. This is because both algorithms visit each node and edge at most once during traversal.
o	Space Complexity:
	DFS: The space complexity for DFS is O(n), where n is the depth of the tree or graph (since it uses a stack to store nodes). In the worst case, it can go as deep as the number of nodes.
	BFS: The space complexity for BFS is O(n) as well, where n is the number of nodes at the current depth level, since it uses a queue to store nodes yet to be explored. In the worst case, BFS may need to store all nodes at a given depth level before moving to the next level.
o	Completeness:
	DFS: DFS is complete if the search tree is finite. It guarantees a solution if one exists.
	BFS: BFS is complete for finite graphs, as it will always find a solution if one exists (since it explores all nodes at a level before moving to the next).
o	Optimality:
	DFS: DFS is not optimal, meaning it does not guarantee the shortest path, especially in graphs with multiple paths.
	BFS: BFS is optimal if all edges have the same cost, as it will always find the shortest path in an unweighted graph.
